{"status":"ready","summary":"Fixed the review finding: `perfect_ht_threshold` now limits perfect hashing again. In CanUsePerfectHashAggregate (plan_aggregate.cpp), the 18-bit/4MB-per-thread extension only applies while the setting is at its default (compared with StringUtil::ToUnsigned(PerfectHtThresholdSetting::DefaultValue)). When the user changes the value, it is a hard upper bound on bits, so 0 or 1 turns perfect hashing off. The lazy-initialisation changes in perfect_aggregate_hashtable.cpp/.hpp from the earlier attempt are unchanged; the reviewer found no problem with them. States are initialised on first touch in AddChunk and Combine, there is a touched counter with an early return in Combine, Scan and Combine skip unset flags 8 bytes at a time, and Destroy only visits set slots.

The build passes. There is no unittest runner here, so I could not run the three requested test files directly. Instead I reproduced their plan checks with `dbe-tool sql` on a 16-bit no-stats key: at the default it plans PERFECT_HASH_GROUP_BY, with threshold 0 or 1 it plans HASH_GROUP_BY (what perfect_ht.test lines 17-33 and test_filter_clause:428 rely on), and with threshold 20 it plans perfect (what test_perfect_ht.test sets). I also read the other plan-regex tests (union_single_dedup, issue8505); they still match because `HASH_GROUP_BY` is a substring of `PERFECT_HASH_GROUP_BY`.

Full bench against the current best: geomean -3.0% (an earlier quick run gave +0.6%, with q03 and q14 noisy). q13 is about -19% on both full runs (95->77ms), q09 -8%, q17 -6.8%, q18 -3.6%. q14 showed +12% and +24% in two runs, but its plan has no grouped aggregate, and a third run gave -1.8%, so that was noise.","generality":"Helps GROUP BY on integer keys whose stats range needs 13-18 bits (customer, part, supplier and date keys) when perfect_ht_threshold is at its default, as long as the per-thread table (2^bits * (state width + 1 flag byte)) is at most 4MB. Lazy initialisation also makes existing <=12-bit perfect aggregates cheaper on tiny inputs. Does not apply when the user has changed perfect_ht_threshold (that value is then the hard bit cap), and not to DISTINCT aggregates, grouping sets, or non-integer keys. Could be slower when the stats range is wide but actual groups are sparse and the input is large.","notes":"The extension now applies only while the setting is at its default value. If a user explicitly sets 12 (the default), it is treated as default and the extension applies; I accepted that because the engine does not expose whether a setting was explicitly set. The harness gives no way to run sqllogictests, so those checks were reproduced with dbe-tool sql. Bench noise is about ±10% per query on quick runs, and the geomean landed at -3.0%, close to the 2.7% bar. The real gain is concentrated in q13. Possible follow-up: tune the 4MB cap, or base acceptance on observed cardinality at runtime."}
